1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,003
Language models now work in teams.

2
00:00:03,353 --> 00:00:06,836
How should a team of hundreds, or a thousand, be organized?

3
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:14,629
Most multi-agent systems rely on a coordinator:
one agent that reads every report and gives every order.

4
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:21,213
Honeybee swarms, ant colonies and bird flocks coordinate
through local interactions, with no one directing the whole.

5
00:00:21,563 --> 00:00:26,103
Do their mechanisms still help language-model
agents when a coordinator is available?

6
00:00:27,533 --> 00:00:30,622
A honeybee swarm chooses its new home without a leader.

7
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:35,778
Scouts inspect cavities themselves,
and the swarm commits once enough of them gather at one.

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,963
Ants mark the paths they walk, and the marks fade.

9
00:00:40,313 --> 00:00:43,947
A shorter path is renewed faster, so the colony settles on it.

10
00:00:45,367 --> 00:00:48,316
In a flock, each bird follows only its nearest neighbours.

11
00:00:48,666 --> 00:00:51,870
A few birds that know the way can
guide the rest past the hawks.

12
00:00:53,300 --> 00:01:01,450
We tested a coordinator against designs built on these mechanisms,
in three test beds with up to 1,024 language-model agents.

13
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:08,997
Here are two recorded episodes
of a wildfire rescue, each with 128 drones.

14
00:01:09,347 --> 00:01:12,319
On the left, a coordinator assigns every drone.

15
00:01:12,669 --> 00:01:17,313
On the right, NatureSwarm: each drone
decides from what nearby drones relay.

16
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:25,444
With 128 drones, coordinators run by smaller
language models saved 41% of victims.

17
00:01:25,794 --> 00:01:30,844
Drones choosing their own tasks,
as social insects do, saved 79%.

18
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:38,815
A simple law fitted on small teams predicted the coordinator's
decline in larger teams for six of seven models.

19
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:46,410
Here a frontier model runs the coordinator of 512 drones,
and a small model runs NatureSwarm.

20
00:01:47,833 --> 00:01:56,309
With 1,024 drones, NatureSwarm run by a small model came close
to a frontier model's coordinator at under a third of the price.

21
00:01:57,733 --> 00:02:04,978
When model calls take time, the fire does not wait: drones under
a frontier model's coordinator hover until their orders arrive.

22
00:02:05,328 --> 00:02:10,878
In teams of 32 and 64 drones, NatureSwarm
then outperformed even that coordinator.

23
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:17,675
NatureSwarm also kept teams working when
radio range was cut or the base fell silent.

24
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:25,613
The third test bed is an epidemic response: county health
departments place testing teams as a new variant spreads.

25
00:02:25,963 --> 00:02:30,921
Counties that pooled alerts with their commuting partners
curbed the variant more than a state coordinator.

26
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:37,244
When we let organizations evolve,
smaller models' populations dropped the coordinator.

27
00:02:37,594 --> 00:02:41,251
The frontier model's population, with 16 drones, kept it.

28
00:02:42,667 --> 00:02:45,534
Every animation in this video is a recorded episode.

29
00:02:45,884 --> 00:02:51,910
The Explorer on the project page plays them
step by step, across all three test beds.

30
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:57,629
Mechanisms borrowed from nature help
where central control reaches its limits.

31
00:02:57,979 --> 00:03:03,378
The test beds, the designs, the experiment scripts
and the Explorer are released with the paper.
